From ffa4e1d39e72441cf07bb0870b9ecafc2103e567 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 11:46:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Always set reader time as UTC POSIXct

raster-era raadtools assigned every time axis through getZ(), which was
POSIXct by construction, so downstream code could count on seconds since
the epoch. terra keeps whatever class it is handed and records a matching
step, so `terra::time(r) <- as.Date(files$date)` gives a Date back with a
"days" step: as.integer() on it counts days rather than seconds, and any
time of day in the catalogue is discarded on the way in.

Add an internal replacement function `.utctime<-` in R/utils.R that routes
the value through timedateFrom(), and use it at all 34 sites that set a
reader's time. Twenty-one of those were wrapping the value in as.Date();
that wrapper is now gone. The thirteen that already passed POSIXct through
are unchanged in behaviour but go through the same door.

This is what tests/testthat/testthat-timezone-modify.R measures. Its
fixture is unclass()ed POSIXct, so with the Date-valued readers it saw
4261 where it wanted 368150400 (readsst), and likewise for readice,
readcurr and readssh. readwind and readamps_d1wind were already POSIXct
and already passed - the AMPS reference value 1445774400 is
2015-10-25 12:00:00 UTC, a stamp as.Date() would have truncated.

No exported object changes, so this needs no roxygenise.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FxfVsMKWJce64vRsZgurfB
